We are grateful to the Reviewers for their time spent considering our work, and thrilled to have received encouraging comments. Among them, Reviewer `T6sp` described our work as **“principled,”** and Reviewer `foRW` found that our approach addressed plugin DSE’s underestimation **“effectively”** in a **“simple and interpretable”** manner. Several Reviewers highlighted our evaluation approach as **“sound methodologically”** (`T6sp`),  **“more reliable”** (`SfCH`), **“thoughtful”** (`RUVy`), and including **“one of the best discussions of how to evaluate UQ methods I have seen”** (`RUVy`). Finally, Reviewers `SfCH` and `RUVy` referred to the writing itself, finding our paper **“easy to understand and well-structured”** and **“clear and well-written.”**

We also received constructive questions and comments and thank the Reviewers for their patience, as we prepared a thorough response to their feedback and our own inquiries. Our updated submission includes **increased experimental scale** to include a 12B-parameter model (Gemma-3-12B), **five extra baselines** (HDBSCAN and four DBSCAN variations) in the semantic clustering evaluation, **four additional figures** (Figures 6, 8, 9, 11), **supplemental analysis** (Appendix Section E) examining the practical distinctions between SE and plugin DSE, and various textual improvements. After repeating experiments with the larger model and correcting a plotting error, our core findings are:
- **Plugin DSE underestimates the “true” semantic entropy**, on average, for typical sample sizes (Figure 2);
- **Our proposed hybrid estimator tends to do so by less** (Figures 2, 8, 9), resulting in more accurate estimation relative to $SE^*$ (Table 1);
- **Our hybrid entropy estimator outperforms other explicit semantic entropy estimators**, including plugin DSE and white-box SE, on the incorrectness detection task (Figure 3);
- **Alphabet size estimation can perform surprisingly well at incorrectness detection**, and two alphabet size estimators (hybrid alphabet size and the related $U_{EigV}$) perform closely to KLE, with all three including the top position in their 95% rank CIs ([1,4], [1, 3], and [1, 3], respectively). This is slightly updated from our original statement, which reported hybrid alphabet size and KLE sharing the top rank interval and $U_{EigV}$ lower, but it strengthens our claim that alphabet size estimation can be a simple, interpretable, and surprisingly effective UQ method in its own right.

We would like to reiterate our appreciation for the reviewers’ time and consideration. We have found their insight invaluable in this process.
